       The ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn() and ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp() functions are used to create a new
       child  process  that  executes  a specified file.  These functions were
       specified by POSIX to provide a standardized  method  of  creating  new
       processes  on  machines that lack the capability to support the ffoorrkk(2)
       system call.  These machines  are  generally  small,  embedded  systems
       lacking MMU support.

       The  ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn() and ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp() functions provide the functional‐
       ity of a combined ffoorrkk(2) and eexxeecc(3), with some optional  housekeeping
       steps in the child process before the eexxeecc(3).  These functions are not
       meant to replace the ffoorrkk(2) and eexxeeccvvee(2) system calls.  In fact, they
       provide  only a subset of the functionality that can be achieved by us‐
       ing the system calls.

       The only difference between ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn()  and  ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp()  is  the
       manner  in  which  they  specify  the  file to be executed by the child
       process.  With ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn(), the executable file  is  specified  as  a
       pathname (which can be absolute or relative).  With ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp(), the
       executable  file is specified as a simple filename; the system searches
       for this file in the list of directories specified by PPAATTHH (in the same
       way as for eexxeeccvvpp(3)).  For the remainder of this page, the  discussion
       is  phrased  in  terms  of  ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn(),  with the understanding that
       ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp() differs only on the point just described.

