       In between the ffoorrkk(()) and the eexxeecc(()) steps, a child process may need to
       perform  a  set  of  housekeeping  actions.   The   ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn()   and
       ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp()  functions  support  a small, well‐defined set of system
       tasks that the child process can accomplish before it executes the exe‐
       cutable file.  These operations are controlled by the attributes object
       pointed to by _a_t_t_r_p and the file actions object pointed to by  _f_i_l_e___a_c_‐
       _t_i_o_n_s.  In the child, processing is done in the following sequence:

       (1)  Process  attribute  actions: signal mask, signal default handlers,
            scheduling algorithm and parameters, process group, and  effective
            user  and group IDs are changed as specified by the attributes ob‐
            ject pointed to by _a_t_t_r_p.

       (2)  File actions, as specified in the _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s argument, are  per‐
            formed  in  the  order that they were specified using calls to the
            ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn__ffiillee__aaccttiioonnss__aadddd**() functions.

       (3)  File descriptors with the FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag set are closed.

       All process attributes in the child, other than those affected  by  at‐
       tributes  specified  in the object pointed to by _a_t_t_r_p and the file ac‐
       tions in the object pointed to by _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s,  will  be  affected  as
       though  the  child was created with ffoorrkk(2) and it executed the program
       with eexxeeccvvee(2).

       The process attributes actions are defined  by  the  attributes  object
       pointed  to by _a_t_t_r_p.  The _s_p_a_w_n_‐_f_l_a_g_s attribute (set using ppoossiixx__ssppaaww‐‐
       nnaattttrr__sseettffllaaggss(3)) controls the general actions that occur,  and  other
       attributes  in  the  object  specify values to be used during those ac‐
       tions.

       The effects of the flags that may be specified in  _s_p_a_w_n_‐_f_l_a_g_s  are  as
       follows:

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSIIGGMMAASSKK
              Set  the  signal  mask to the signal set specified in the _s_p_a_w_n_‐
              _s_i_g_m_a_s_k attribute of the object pointed to  by  _a_t_t_r_p.   If  the
              PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSIIGGMMAASSKK  flag is not set, then the child inherits
              the parent’s signal mask.

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSIIGGDDEEFF
              Reset the disposition of all signals in the set specified in the
              _s_p_a_w_n_‐_s_i_g_d_e_f_a_u_l_t attribute of the object pointed to by _a_t_t_r_p  to
              the  default.   For the treatment of the dispositions of signals
              not specified in the _s_p_a_w_n_‐_s_i_g_d_e_f_a_u_l_t attribute, or  the  treat‐
              ment when PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSIIGGDDEEFF is not specified, see eexxeeccvvee(2).

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSCCHHEEDDPPAARRAAMM
              If  this  flag  is set, and the PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSCCHHEEDDUULLEERR flag is
              not set, then set the scheduling parameters  to  the  parameters
              specified  in  the  _s_p_a_w_n_‐_s_c_h_e_d_p_a_r_a_m  attribute  of  the  object
              pointed to by _a_t_t_r_p.

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSCCHHEEDDUULLEERR
              Set the scheduling policy algorithm and parameters of the child,
              as follows:

              •  The scheduling policy is set to the value  specified  in  the
                 _s_p_a_w_n_‐_s_c_h_e_d_p_o_l_i_c_y  attribute  of the object pointed to by _a_t_‐
                 _t_r_p.

              •  The scheduling parameters are set to the value  specified  in
                 the  _s_p_a_w_n_‐_s_c_h_e_d_p_a_r_a_m  attribute  of the object pointed to by
                 _a_t_t_r_p (but see BUGS).

              If the PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSCCHHEEDDPPAARRAAMM and  PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSCCHHEEDDPPOOLLIICCYY
              flags  are  not  specified, the child inherits the corresponding
              scheduling attributes from the parent.

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__RREESSEETTIIDDSS
              If this flag is set, reset the effective UID and GID to the real
              UID and GID of the parent process.  If this  flag  is  not  set,
              then  the child retains the effective UID and GID of the parent.
              In either case, if the set‐user‐ID and  set‐group‐ID  permission
              bits are enabled on the executable file, their effect will over‐
              ride the setting of the effective UID and GID (se eexxeeccvvee(2)).

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTPPGGRROOUUPP
              Set the process group to the value specified in the _s_p_a_w_n_‐_p_g_r_o_u_p
              attribute  of  the  object  pointed  to by _a_t_t_r_p.  If the _s_p_a_w_n_‐
              _p_g_r_o_u_p attribute has the value 0, the child’s process  group  ID
              is  made  the  same as its process ID.  If the PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTPP‐‐
              GGRROOUUPP flag is not set, the child inherits the  parent’s  process
              group ID.

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__UUSSEEVVFFOORRKK
              Since glibc 2.24, this flag has no effect.  On older implementa‐
              tions,  setting this flag forces the ffoorrkk(()) step to use vvffoorrkk(2)
              instead of ffoorrkk(2).  The __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE feature test macro must  be
              defined to obtain the definition of this constant.

       PPOOSSIIXX__SSPPAAWWNN__SSEETTSSIIDD (since glibc 2.26)
              If  this  flag is set, the child process shall create a new ses‐
              sion and become the session leader.   The  child  process  shall
              also become the process group leader of the new process group in
              the session (see sseettssiidd(2)).  The __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE feature test macro
              must be defined to obtain the definition of this constant.

       If  _a_t_t_r_p  is NULL, then the default behaviors described above for each
       flag apply.

       The _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s argument specifies a sequence of file operations  that
       are  performed  in  the  child process after the general processing de‐
       scribed above, and before it performs the eexxeecc(3).  If _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s  is
       NULL,  then  no special action is taken, and standard eexxeecc(3) semantics
       apply—file descriptors open before the exec  remain  open  in  the  new
       process, except those for which the FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag has been set.  File
       locks remain in place.

       If  _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s  is  not  NULL, then it contains an ordered set of re‐
       quests to ooppeenn(2), cclloossee(2), and dduupp22(2)  files.   These  requests  are
       added   to  the  _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s  by  ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn__ffiillee__aaccttiioonnss__aaddddooppeenn(3),
       ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn__ffiillee__aaccttiioonnss__aaddddcclloossee(3), and  ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn__ffiillee__aaccttiioonnss__aadd‐‐
       dddduupp22(3).   The  requested  operations  are performed in the order they
       were added to _f_i_l_e___a_c_t_i_o_n_s.

       If any of the housekeeping actions fails (due  to  bogus  values  being
       passed  or  other  reasons  why  signal  handling,  process scheduling,
       process group ID functions, and file descriptor operations might fail),
       the child process exits with exit value 127.

   eexxeecc(()) sstteepp
       Once the child has successfully forked and performed all requested pre‐
       exec steps, the child runs the requested executable.

       The child process takes its environment from the _e_n_v_p  argument,  which
       is interpreted as if it had been passed to eexxeeccvvee(2).  The arguments to
       the  created process come from the _a_r_g_v argument, which is processed as
       for eexxeeccvvee(2).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       Upon successful completion, ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn() and ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp() place  the
       PID  of  the  child process in _p_i_d, and return 0.  If there is an error
       during the ffoorrkk(()) step, then no child is created, the contents of  _*_p_i_d
       are  unspecified,  and  these  functions  return an error number as de‐
       scribed below.

       Even when these functions return a success status,  the  child  process
       may still fail for a plethora of reasons related to its pre‐eexxeecc() ini‐
       tialization.   In  addition,  the  eexxeecc(3)  may  fail.  In all of these
       cases, the child process will exit with the exit value of 127.

EERRRROORRSS
       The ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn() and ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnnpp() functions fail only  in  the  case
       where  the  underlying  ffoorrkk(2),  vvffoorrkk(2), or cclloonnee(2) call fails;  in
       these cases, these functions return an error number, which will be  one
       of the errors described for ffoorrkk(2), vvffoorrkk(2), or cclloonnee(2).

