       The  program  below  demonstrates  the  use of various functions in the
       POSIX spawn API.  The program accepts command‐line attributes that  can
       be  used  to create file actions and attributes objects.  The remaining
       command‐line arguments are used as the executable name and command‐line
       arguments of the program that is executed in the child.

       In the first run, the ddaattee(1) command is executed in the child, and the
       ppoossiixx__ssppaawwnn() call employs no file actions or attributes objects.

           $ ..//aa..oouutt ddaattee;
           PID of child: 7634
           Tue Feb  1 19:47:50 CEST 2011
           Child status: exited, status=0

       In the next run, the _-_c command‐line option is used to  create  a  file
       actions object that closes standard output in the child.  Consequently,
       ddaattee(1)  fails when trying to perform output and exits with a status of
       1.

           $ ..//aa..oouutt --cc ddaattee;
           PID of child: 7636
           date: write error: Bad file descriptor
           Child status: exited, status=1

       In the next run, the _-_s command‐line option is used to  create  an  at‐
       tributes  object  that  specifies  that  all (blockable) signals in the
       child should be blocked.  Consequently, trying to kill child  with  the
       default signal sent by kkiillll(1) (i.e., SSIIGGTTEERRMM) fails, because that sig‐
       nal  is  blocked.   Therefore,  to kill the child, SSIIGGKKIILLLL is necessary
       (SSIIGGKKIILLLL can’t be blocked).

           $ ..//aa..oouutt --ss sslleeeepp 6600 &&
           [1] 7637
           $ PID of child: 7638

       $ kkiillll 77663388;
       $ kkiillll --KKIILLLL 77663388;
       $ Child status: killed by signal 9
       [1]+  Done                    ./a.out -s sleep 60

       When we try to execute a nonexistent command in the child, the  eexxeecc(3)
       fails and the child exits with a status of 127.

           $ ..//aa..oouutt xxxxxxxxxx;
